The de-Broglie equation is
$$
\begin{aligned}
\lambda &=\frac{h}{m v}=\frac{h}{\sqrt{2 m k}} \Rightarrow m=\frac{h}{\lambda v} \\
\Rightarrow \quad m \propto \frac{1}{\lambda}, \text { if } \frac{h}{v} &=\mathrm{constant}
\end{aligned}
$$
Again, $\frac{h}{v}$ will be constant only when $v$ is constant
Thus, in present case the de-Broglie wavelength of heavier particle will be smaller. When the velocities of two particles will be same or both move with same kinetic energy(K).
